.TH SHARKY 1 "2026-11-02" "sharky 3.5.4" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
sharky \- Unix-first interactive shell
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B sharky
[\fIOPTIONS\fR]
.br
.B sharky
\fB\-c\fR \fICOMMAND\fR
.br
.B sharky
\fIFILE\fR [\fIARG\fR...]
.br
.B sharky
.B state
\fB\-\-json\fR [\fB\-\-env\fR]
.br
.B sharky
.B config explain
[\fITOPIC\fR [\fIWORD\fR...]]
.br
.B sharky
.B status
.br
.B sharky
.B doctor
.br
.B sharky
.B help
[\fITOPIC\fR [\fIWORD\fR...]]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B sharky
is an interactive POSIX-like shell written in Rust. Commands run as real Unix
processes; the parser, executor, line editor, prompt, history and completion are
owned by the shell itself.
.PP
With no arguments and a terminal on standard input, sharky starts interactively.
With no arguments and no terminal it reads commands from standard input.
.PP
sharky is in alpha, an ongoing stage of maturity delivered through the normal
release channel. Use it as an interactive shell; using it as a login shell is
not recommended during alpha.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B \-c \fICOMMAND\fR
Run \fICOMMAND\fR and exit with its status.
.TP
.B \-\-check
Parse \fB\-c\fR or a file for syntax only: exit 0 silently on success, or 2 with
a line reference on a top-level syntax error. Requires \fB\-c\fR or a file;
standard input is not supported. Only top-level syntax is promised, nested
bodies are not guaranteed to be checked. Nothing is executed, and configuration,
profiles and history are neither read nor written. Cannot be combined with
\fB\-i\fR, \fB\-l\fR, \fB\-\-json\fR or \fBstate\fR.
.TP
.B \-i
Force interactive mode; fails when standard input or output is not a terminal.
.TP
.B \-l, \-\-login
Read login profiles before the command or system input. Not recommended as a
login shell during alpha.
.TP
.B \-P, \-\-private
Use no history file: prior commands are not read and new ones are not written.
.TP
.B \-\-json
Print one JSON object for \fB\-c\fR or \fBstate\fR.
.TP
.B \-\-env
Include the environment in \fBstate \-\-json\fR.
.TP
.B state
Print schema version, cwd, last status and pid as JSON (requires \fB\-\-json\fR).
.TP
.B config explain
Print the configuration guide as plain text. With no \fITOPIC\fR, print an index
of topic ids; otherwise join the words after \fBconfig explain\fR with spaces and
print that topic. Each parser directive has its own topic, alongside \fBtide history\fR,
\fBfiles\fR, \fBtheme\fR and \fBoverwrite\fR. Unknown or malformed
requests exit 2. Runs before the shell starts, so no configuration, profiles or
history are read.
.TP
.B status
Print plain-text facts, one per line: the sharky version, the resolved config
and theme paths with whether each file exists, the history path with whether it
exists, and the active compile-time feature flags (\fBnone\fR in this release).
The paths follow \fBSHARKY_CONFIG\fR, \fBXDG_CONFIG_HOME\fR and \fBHOME\fR for
configuration and \fBSHARKY_HISTORY\fR, \fBXDG_DATA_HOME\fR and \fBHOME\fR for
history. Always exits 0. Never prints environment values, configuration values
or \fBpearl\fR directives, and never writes files.
.TP
.B doctor
Run read-only local checks and print one \fBOK\fR or \fBFAIL\fR line per check:
validity of standard input and output descriptors (the reason states whether
each is a terminal; a pipe is fine), parsing of \fBconfig.sharky\fR through the
configuration parser, parsing of \fBtheme.sharky\fR, history file permissions and
writability, and availability of the baseline external tools \fBsh\fR, \fBls\fR
and \fBcat\fR on \fBPATH\fR. Unreadable config files are reported separately
because the parser silently skips them. The history file is only inspected via
metadata: it is never opened for append and no lock is taken. Exits 0 when all
checks pass and 1 when any fails. No fstab checks are performed.
.TP
.B \-\-no\-config
Do not read the configuration files.
.TP
.B \-\-version
Print the version and exit.
.TP
.B \-\-help
Print a short usage summary and exit.
.TP
.B help \fI[TOPIC [WORD...]]\fR
With no \fITOPIC\fR, print the same text as \fB\-\-help\fR. With one, join the
words after \fBhelp\fR with spaces and print that topic. Topics are builtin
names, command-line flags and subcommands, and the configuration topics of
\fBconfig explain\fR. An unknown topic exits 1. Runs before the shell starts, so
no configuration is read or created.
.SH LANGUAGE
The prompt, \fB\-c\fR and script files share one grammar:
.IP \(bu 2
single and double quotes, backslash escapes and \fB#\fR comments;
.IP \(bu 2
variables, positional parameters, \fB${NAME:\-default}\fR operators,
substrings, case conversion, indirect expansion and \fB$(...)\fR command
substitution;
.IP \(bu 2
indexed and associative arrays, including slices and quoted \fB"${a[@]}"\fR;
.IP \(bu 2
arithmetic expansion \fB$((...))\fR and C-style \fBfor ((...))\fR loops;
.IP \(bu 2
tilde expansion and glob patterns;
.IP \(bu 2
\fBshopt \-s globstar\fR enables recursive \fB**\fR matching (off by default),
\fBshopt \-u globstar\fR restores single\-level globbing, and
\fBshopt globstar\fR reports \fBon\fR or \fBoff\fR;
.IP \(bu 2
pipelines, redirections (\fB>\fR, \fB>>\fR, \fB<\fR, \fB2>&1\fR), \fB;\fR,
\fB&&\fR and \fB||\fR lists, process substitution (\fB<(list)\fR,
\fB>(list)\fR), \fBexec\fR and \fBtime\fR;
.IP \(bu 2
\fBif\fR, \fBfor\fR, \fBwhile\fR, \fBuntil\fR, \fBcase\fR (including
\fB;;&\fR and \fB;&\fR), shell functions, groups, subshells, extglob patterns
and \fB!\fR.
.PP
Unquoted expansions split on \fBIFS\fR (space, tab and newline by default):
repeated delimiters keep empty fields, leading and trailing ones are dropped,
and \fBIFS=\fR disables splitting. A quoted \fB"$*"\fR, \fB"${a[*]}"\fR or
\fB"${!a[*]}"\fR joins fields with the first character of \fBIFS\fR, or nothing
when \fBIFS\fR is empty. Quoted expansions are never split.
.PP
This is a subset of Bash, not a replacement. Unsupported syntax fails with
status 2 instead of falling back to another shell.
.SH CONFIGURATION
Configuration lives in \fB$SHARKY_CONFIG\fR, or
\fB${XDG_CONFIG_HOME:\-~/.config}/sharky\fR. Two files are read, each a small
declarative language with one statement per line, \fB//\fR comments and
double-quoted values:
.PP
.nf
    tide greeting off
    tide history 10000
    pearl EDITOR "nvim"
    current "~/.local/bin"
    reef ll "ls -lah"
    anchor ctrl+r history_search
.fi
.PP
.SS config.sharky
.TP
.B tide \fIkey value\fR
Shell preferences: \fBgreeting\fR, \fBautosuggest\fR, \fBhighlight\fR,
\fBstarship\fR and \fBprofile_paths\fR (\fBon\fR or \fBoff\fR) and
\fBhistory\fR (a number).
.TP
.B pearl \fINAME value\fR
Set an environment variable.
.TP
.B current \fIdirectory\fR
Prepend a directory to \fBPATH\fR.
.TP
.B reef \fIname command\fR
Define an alias.
.TP
.B anchor \fIkey action\fR
Bind a key to an editor action. Key names use \fBctrl+x\fR, \fBc+x\fR,
\fBalt+x\fR or \fBm+x\fR, named keys such as \fBtab\fR, \fBshift-tab\fR,
\fBhome\fR and \fBend\fR, or a single character. Actions include
\fBaccept_line\fR, \fBabort\fR, \fBbackward_char\fR, \fBforward_char\fR,
\fBbackward_word\fR, \fBforward_word\fR, \fBkill_line\fR, \fByank\fR,
\fBundo\fR, \fBhistory_search\fR, \fBcomplete\fR,
\fBaccept_suggestion\fR and \fBfuzzy_files\fR.
.SS theme.sharky
.TP
.B coral \fIname #RRGGBB\fR
Define a colour.
.TP
.B wave \fIkey value\fR
Set the prompt template (\fBprompt\fR) and styles. Styles reference colours with
\fB@name\fR, for example \fBwave prompt.cwd @kelp\fR.
.PP
Unknown instructions, wrong arity, invalid colours and undefined colour
references are reported as \fBfile:line\fR errors; the shell keeps running with
its defaults.
.PP
.B sharky config explain
prints this guidance as plain text: \fBsharky config explain\fR lists the topic
ids and \fBsharky config explain tide history\fR prints one topic. There is no
top-level \fBsharky explain\fR alias.
.SH KEYS
.TP
.B Tab
Complete the current word. Several candidates insert the common prefix and
open an inline menu; Tab and Shift-Tab move, Enter inserts and Esc closes.
.TP
.B Right, End
Accept the autosuggest ghost text when the cursor is at the end of the line.
.TP
.B Ctrl-T
Open the fuzzy file list. Type to filter, arrows move, Enter inserts and Esc
cancels without changing the buffer.
.TP
.B Ctrl-R
Incremental history search. Enter runs the match and Ctrl-G cancels.
.TP
.B Ctrl-_
Undo the last edit.
.TP
.B Up, Down, Home, End
Move between buffer lines and within the current line. Bracketed paste is
inserted verbatim; Enter submits the whole buffer.
.PP
With
.B set -o vi
the editor uses vi bindings: Esc enters normal mode; h, l, 0, $, w, b, e, j and
k move; x, dd, dw, d$, D, cw, C, p, P, u and r<char> edit. A count prefix is
accepted. Esc cancels a pending operator, Enter submits and Backspace edits.
.SH LIMITATIONS
ANSI-C escapes above 0x7f are stored as UTF-8 characters, not raw bytes.
.PP
A process substitution created inside a pipeline is reaped by the process that
created it, so the top-level
.B wait
does not observe it. Direct substitutions are tracked and reaped.
.PP
.B TIMEFORMAT
is not supported; use
.B time
or
.B time -p.
.PP
.B shopt
accepts only the
.B globstar
option. Combined or interleaved \fB\-s\fR/\fB\-u\fR flags and other option
names are rejected, and the setting is not persisted across shells. Matching
paths are deduplicated; Bash can repeat paths for overlapping nonconsecutive
globstars.
.PP
Leading \fB./\fR and \fB../\fR components are preserved in globstar results. A
\fB.\fR or \fB..\fR component later in the path, such as
\fBdir/./**/*.txt\fR, is not expanded and the pattern stays literal.
.PP
.SH HISTORY
After each command is evaluated, sharky appends it to one shared history file
under a lock. A failed write is reported and the entry stays only in memory; a
torn tail from a crash is dropped and cleared on the next append. A shell that
started earlier keeps its own view until it runs
.B history merge
from the prompt; this never rewrites entries from another session. Private mode
(\fB\-P\fR) keeps history in memory and leaves the file untouched. A history file
that cannot be read warns at startup and makes \fBhistory \-c\fR fail instead of
claiming it cleared the file.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I ~/.config/sharky/config.sharky
Shell preferences, environment, PATH entries, aliases and key bindings.
.TP
.I ~/.config/sharky/theme.sharky
Colour palette and prompt styles.
.TP
.I ~/.local/share/sharky/history
Command history, limited by \fBtide history\fR. \fBSHARKY_HISTORY\fR overrides
the path; otherwise \fBXDG_DATA_HOME\fR replaces the \fB~/.local/share\fR prefix.
.TP
.I /etc/sharky/profile.sharky, ~/.config/sharky/profile.sharky
Login profiles run by \fB\-l\fR.
.TP
.I /etc/profile, ~/.profile, ~/.bash_profile, ~/.zshprofile
Login mode imports only literal \fINAME=value\fR and
\fBexport \fINAME=value\fR lines from these files.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B SHARKY_CONFIG
Configuration directory, overriding the default.
.TP
.B SHARKY_HISTORY
History file path, overriding the default.
.TP
.B XDG_CONFIG_HOME
Used to find the configuration directory.
.TP
.B XDG_DATA_HOME
Used to find the history file.
.TP
.B NO_COLOR
Disables colour in the prompt when set.
.TP
.B PATH
Searched for commands, extended by \fBcurrent\fR entries.
.SH EXIT STATUS
The status of the last command, or the argument of \fBexit\fR. A syntax error
exits with 2; a command that cannot be found exits with 127; a command that
cannot be executed exits with 126. \fBstatus\fR always exits 0, \fBdoctor\fR
exits 1 when a check fails, and malformed subcommand arguments exit 2. An
unknown \fBhelp\fR topic exits 1.
.SH EXAMPLES
.nf
    sharky -c 'printf "a b\\n" | wc -w'
    sharky script.sh argument
    sharky -l -c 'echo "$LOGIN_VAR"'
    sharky --json -c 'printf hi'
    sharky state --json --env
    sharky status
    sharky doctor
    sharky
.fi
